  ],
  "text": "Coal is sunlight from three hundred million years ago. We started spending it all at once. Britain was running out of wood. Forests were coming down faster than they grew back. Coal held far more energy per kilogram, and it was sitting right there underground. So they dug. And the deeper they dug, the more water came in. Which makes the pivot of modern history a plumbing problem. You cannot mine a flooded shaft, and hauling water by hand costs more than the coal is worth. So there was suddenly a fortune waiting for anyone who could pump water without muscle. The steam engine was built to do that. Not transport. Not factories. Drainage. It burned coal to lift water so that more coal could be dug up and burned. Then somebody noticed it could turn anything, and the machine walked out of the mine. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
